      "materials": [
        {
          "week": 10,
          "title": "Closures",
          "notes": "A closure is a function together with the environment where it was made. When a function refers to a variable that is not its parameter (a free variable), the closure keeps that variable alive. Example in Racket: (define (make-adder n) (lambda (x) (+ x n))). (make-adder 5) returns a function that adds 5, because the lambda keeps n = 5. Closures make callbacks, iterators, and partial application possible."
        },
        {
          "week": 11,
          "title": "Environments and evaluation",
          "notes": "An interpreter evaluates an expression in an environment: a map from names to values. eval takes (expression, environment) and returns a value. A number evaluates to itself. A name is looked up in the environment. (let ([x e1]) e2) evaluates e1, then evaluates e2 in a new environment that extends the old one with x. A new environment for each let gives lexical scope."
        },
        {
          "week": 12,
          "title": "Functions in the interpreter",
          "notes": "A lambda evaluates to a closure: (params, body, environment where the lambda was made). To apply a closure, evaluate the arguments, make a new environment that extends the closure's environment with params bound to the arguments, and evaluate the body there. If you extend the caller's environment instead, you get dynamic scope."
        }
      ]
    },
