      "materials": [
        {
          "week": 10,
          "title": "Context-free grammars",
          "notes": "A context-free grammar has variables, terminals, rules (variable -> string of variables and terminals), and a start variable. A derivation replaces one variable at a time. Example: S -> 0S1 | e makes { 0^n 1^n }. A grammar is ambiguous if a string has two different leftmost derivations."
        },
        {
          "week": 11,
          "title": "Pushdown automata",
          "notes": "A pushdown automaton (PDA) is an NFA with a stack. Each step reads an input symbol (or nothing), pops a stack symbol (or nothing), and pushes a string. A PDA for { 0^n 1^n }: push each 0, pop one 0 for each 1, accept when the stack is empty at the end. PDAs recognize exactly the context-free languages."
        }
      ]
